# Line endings. Everything text-like is normalized to LF on the way into the repository and
# checked out with LF on every host, whatever a contributor's core.autocrlf is set to.
#
# This is not cosmetic here: this repository's entire enforcement layer is bash scripts under
# scripts/, and the Dev Container runs Linux no matter what the host is. A script that arrives
# with CRLF line endings fails as 'bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M' — a failure whose message
# names neither the file nor the real cause. .vscode/settings.json sets "files.eol" for the same
# reason, but only for VS Code and only for files it writes; git needs its own rule.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated separately so it survives an edit to the line above: an executable script must never
# gain CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf

# 'text=auto' detects binary content by inspection, so ordinary binary files need no rule here.
# Add an explicit '<pattern> binary' line only for a format that detection gets wrong — a file
# git diffs or normalizes although it must not be touched.
